//? Title: Vector-Quantized Autoencoder: Discrete Codes and Separate Gradient Routes
//? Subtitle: Exact selected-vector values, an explicit surrogate Jacobian and a fitting-only raster prior
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An image code can be a sequence of dictionary symbols rather than a continuous Gaussian
sample. Learning those symbols requires deciding how gradients cross nearest-neighbour
selection and how a decoder generates without a target. This report fits a compact spatial
VQ-VAE on 210 local circle/square images, with 42 validation and 42 test cases. All 21 Adam
updates remain editable C++. Validation reconstruction NLL selects epoch 3; test pixel MSE
0.126616 is worse than the fitting-only mean's 0.048548. Five of sixteen codes are used.
Unfiltered reconstructions and twenty prior decodes retain the short run's failures.
)
\toc

> Snap encoder vectors to a learned dictionary

[Van den Oord, Vinyals and Kavukcuoglu (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.00937)
introduce a discrete bottleneck with a straight-through reconstruction gradient and separate
dictionary and commitment penalties. Here the encoder produces a 7×7 lattice of four-vectors.
Each vector chooses one of sixteen learned entries; forty-nine IDs completely specify the
decoder input. The same lookup and decoder also accept independently generated ID grids.
No image or continuous encoder feature bypasses the discrete bottleneck.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq_vae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(51, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 5.6;
  st.height    = 2.8;
  st.font_size = 20;
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Binary image\n28 x 28 x 1", st)
      .node("e", {7, 0}, "Encoder / Conv-ReLU-avg\n8 then 4 channels", st)
      .node("z", {14, 0}, "Encoder vectors\n7 x 7 x 4", st)
      .node("q", {21, 0}, "Nearest learned entry\nDictionary16 x 4", st)
      .node("i", {28, 0}, "49 discrete IDs\nEach in0..15", st)
      .node("g", {35, 0}, "Lookup / shared decoder\nBernoulli probabilities", st)
      .node("prior", {21, 6}, "Fit-code Markov prior\nExplicit uniform bank", st)
      .node("pi", {28, 6}, "Independent ID grid\nNo image input", st)
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "z")
      .edge("z", "q")
      .edge("q", "i")
      .edge("i", "g")
      .edge("prior", "pi")
      .edge("pi", "g")
      .text(
          {17, -6},
          "Reconstruction: identity SURROGATE to encoder, no dictionary gradient.\nDictionary "
          "penalty moves entries; commitment penalty moves encoder.\nThe prior is fitted "
          "separately on FIT codes after checkpoint selection.",
          20)
      .save_svg("build/vq_vae_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vq_vae_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The encoder's spatial vectors become dictionary IDs. Prior decoding uses IDs alone; the nearest-assignment derivative is deliberately replaced during reconstruction training.)
\alttext(A binary image becomes forty-nine encoder vectors, nearest dictionary entries, discrete IDs and a shared decoder. A separately fitted Markov prior supplies independent ID grids to the same decoder.)

For encoded vectors e_i and book entries b_k, select k_i=argmin_k ||e_i-b_k||² and q_i=b_k_i.
Ties select the lowest code index. Let stop preserve values while stopping all derivatives.
The source-preserving surrogate is

$$
s_i=\operatorname{stop}(q_i)+[e_i-\operatorname{stop}(e_i)],\\
D=\operatorname{mean}_{i,d}[\operatorname{stop}(e_{id})-q_{id}]^2,\qquad
C=\operatorname{mean}_{i,d}[e_{id}-\operatorname{stop}(q_{id})]^2,\\
R_b=\sum_{j=1}^{784}[\operatorname{softplus}(a_{bj})-x_{bj}a_{bj}],\qquad
L=\operatorname{mean}_b R_b+D+0.25C.
$$
\alttext(The forward surrogate equals selected vectors. Dictionary and commitment squares detach opposite targets and average all latent elements. Pixel NLL sums 784 terms before averaging images.)

The reconstruction gradient through s is identity to e and absent to book. Dictionary D
moves only book, while commitment C moves only encoder. This surrogate is not the derivative
of the piecewise-constant nearest assignment. Finite differences of the entire hard forward
therefore cannot validate it; analytic route checks can. The expression avoids cancellation
against a large encoder value, although signed zero can normalize. Available continuous
higher paths follow the surrogate. This teaching model uses gradient dictionary updates,
no EMA, code reset, Gaussian KL or learned neural prior. The chosen reduction over all latent
elements fixes the effective relative weights; it must not silently become a latent sum.

Dense distance work is O(NKD), where N=B×49, K=16 and D=4. One-hot lookup has the same
order and stores N×K assignments. The spatial convolutions cost O(BHW×9C(D+1)), C=8.
The caller owns batch order, optimizer state, checkpoint selection and prior uniforms.

> A nearest tie and three gradient routes small enough to inspect

Vectors (-0.5,0) and (0.9,0.2) choose dictionary entries (-1,0) and (1,0), respectively.
The first vector ties entries 0 and 1, so ID 0 wins. The following code prints reconstruction,
dictionary and quarter-weighted commitment derivatives for every coordinate. The connecting
segments show chosen neighbours, not differentiable paths through argmin.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq_vae_neighbours.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  auto e       = n::tensor({-.5, 0., .9, .2}, {2, 2}, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_(),
       b       = n::tensor({-1., 0., 0., 0., 1., 0.}, {3, 2}, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  const auto q = n::models::VectorQuantize(e, b, 3, 2);
  const auto recon = n::grad(.5 * n::sum(n::square(q.straight_through)) + 0 * n::sum(b), {e, b});
  const auto dictionary = n::grad(q.codebook_loss + 0 * n::sum(e), {e, b}),
             commit     = n::grad(.25 * q.commitment_loss + 0 * n::sum(b), {e, b});
  if (q.indices.to_vector<int>() != std::vector<int>{0, 2} ||
      !n::equal_bits(q.quantized, q.straight_through))
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "| group | coordinate | reconstruction | dictionary | quarter commitment "
      "|\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int group = 0; group < 2; ++group) {
    const auto r = recon[group].to_vector<double>(), d = dictionary[group].to_vector<double>(),
               c = commit[group].to_vector<double>();
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < r.size(); ++j)
      std::print(
          "| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",
          group == 0 ? "encoder" : "book",
          j,
          r[j],
          d[j],
          c[j]);
  }
  auto chart = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  chart.lines({-.5, -1}, {0, 0}, "chosen neighbour", dm::RGBA{100, 116, 139}, 2)
      .lines({.9, 1}, {.2, 0}, "", dm::RGBA{100, 116, 139}, 2)
      .points({-1, 0, 1}, {0, 0, 0}, "dictionary", dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38}, 6)
      .points({-.5, .9}, {0, .2}, "encoder", dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .x_limits(-1.3, 1.7)
      .y_limits(-.15, .45)
      .title("Nearest dictionary vectors / lowest-index ties")
      .x_label("embedding coordinate 1")
      .y_label("embedding coordinate 2")
      .save_svg("build/vq_vae_neighbours.svg");
}
```
