> Fit the code prior separately, then decode IDs alone

For each fitting grid in raster order, reset previous=BOS16 and count transitions.
Half-count smoothing gives p(code|previous)=(count+0.5)/(context_total+8), including a
separate BOS row. This first-order Markov baseline is not the paper's PixelCNN. The prior
knows only the previous symbol, not the full spatial prefix or neighbouring rows.
Its score sums all forty-nine token NLLs before averaging images. Selected fitting/test
prior NLLs are 30.673300410/30.380483406 nats per grid; these are not image log likelihoods.

The program below fits only saved fitting IDs, regenerates all explicit Float64 midpoint
uniforms from seed 2729 and verifies sampled IDs. It then restores only four decoder leaves,
looks up symbols in the saved dictionary and checks all twenty saved probability images.
Neither encoder, target image nor factor label enters decoding. No sample is rejected.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq_vae_priors.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto     a = n::load_npz("build/vq_vae/model_arrays.npz"),
             out   = n::load_npz("build/vq_vae/outputs.npz"),
             saved = n::load_npz("build/vq_vae/prior.npz");
  auto find        = [](const n::NamedTensors& a, const std::string& name) {
    for (const auto& [key, value] : a)
      if (key == name)
        return value;
    throw std::runtime_error(name);
  };
  const n::models::VqMarkovPrior prior(find(out, "fit_index_grid"));
  const auto                     uniforms = n::models::MidpointUniformNoise({20, 49}, 2729),
             ids                          = prior.Sample(uniforms);
  if (!n::equal_bits(prior.counts(), find(saved, "counts")) ||
      !n::equal_bits(prior.probabilities(), find(saved, "probabilities")) ||
      !n::equal_bits(uniforms, find(saved, "uniform")) ||
      !n::equal_bits(ids, find(saved, "sample_ids")))
    return 1;
  n::ParameterStore decoder(2707);
  n::models::VqDecoder(decoder, n::zeros({49, 4}));
  for (const auto& name : decoder.names())
    decoder.set(name, find(a, name));
  if (decoder.size() != 4)
    return 1;
  const auto probability =
      n::sigmoid(n::models::VqDecoder(decoder, n::models::VqLookup(ids, find(a, "codebook"))));
  if (!n::equal_bits(probability, find(saved, "probability")))
    return 1;
  n::save_image_grid(
      probability.reshape({20, 28, 28, 1}), "build/vq_vae_priors.png", 5, 0, 1, 4, 220. / 255., 4);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vq_vae_priors.png)
// result_end

\caption(All twenty unfiltered learned-prior decoder probabilities, five per row, on the same fixed grayscale. The short fit's weak image generation is retained.)
\alttext(Twenty grey prior probability images are shown in their original draw order, without selection for convincing shape structure.)

A fixed-width ID lattice needs 49×4=196 raw symbol bits, excluding dictionary, decoder,
framing and any entropy coder. No finished codec, compression ratio or image-likelihood
claim follows from that arithmetic or from the code-prior score.

> Evidence and limits

The core owns nearest quantization, exact selected-vector forward routing, separate stopped
penalties, spatial inference, code-only decoding, usage counts and the explicit-uniform
raster prior. The report owns data packaging, all updates and selection. An optional
independent NumPy development check verifies every one of the 805 first parameter
surrogate derivatives, the bit-exact first Adam step, saved forwards, all three validation
candidates, complete batch history, every prior count and all prior draws. It never trains
or prepares data:

```
python3 scripts/check_vector_quantized_autoencoder.py docs/mepml/examples/nn/2_autoencoders/2.2_variational_autoencoders/build/vq_vae
```

The run demonstrates faithful discrete bottleneck mechanics. Its short training, five used
codes, weak images and worse-than-mean MSE do not establish realistic generation, semantic
code meaning, a benchmark result or a successful remedy for codebook collapse.
